# Agent-first maintenance

Replace `{{PROJECT_NAME}}` with your project name after cloning this template.

## Before each session

1. Read open GitHub feature issues (`label:feature`) and `docs/FEATURES.md` for phase context
2. Follow `.cursor/rules/github-workflow.mdc` — verify issue, phase, spec, and branch before coding
3. Read `AGENTS.md` for project conventions and commands
4. Run the project test command before invasive changes (define `test` in `package.json`, e.g. `pnpm run test`)

## During work

- Work only on the phase/feature indicated in `docs/FEATURES.md`, the linked GitHub issue, and your `phase-<n>/` branch
- Prefer small diffs, early returns, and self-explanatory names over comments
- Do not skip phases defined in `docs/FEATURES.md`
- Add stack-specific conventions in additional `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` files (language, imports, UI locale, etc.)

## End of session

1. Update the linked GitHub issue and feature spec in `docs/features/F*.md`
2. Set `status` in `scripts/github-tasks.manifest.json` when a feature completes (CI syncs issues on merge to `main`)
3. Update phase completion in `docs/FEATURES.md` when a phase closes
4. Add a row to the **Handoff log** in `docs/FEATURES.md` with a concrete next step
5. Open or update PR with `Closes #N`; merge via `main` (see `github-workflow.mdc`)
6. Run tests (and build if application code changed)
7. Follow `commit-convention.mdc` when marking features complete

## Commands (template)

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm run sync:github-tasks:dry-run
pnpm run sync:github-tasks
```

Add your app commands (`dev`, `test`, `build`) to `package.json` and document them in `AGENTS.md` / `docs/FEATURES.md`.
